Files and scripts in this directory are in charge on creating a list of
"faux" packages for britney. These are packages that do not exist in the
archive, but which we want britney to think they exist. At the moment
they are used for:

  - allowing packages in contrib to migrate even if their dependencies
    are not satisfied within the archive. This is done by creating a
    faux package for each missing dependency.

  - ensure some packages do not get accidentally removed, because they
    are needed by parts of testing which don't declare a Depends on
    them. In particular, to ensure packages needed by the installer and
    tasksel are not removed. This is done by creating faux packages that
    do depend on all packages which are not to be removed.

To create packages of the first kind, just append suitable entries to
the toplevel FauxPackages file. These should have at least a Package and
Version fields. You can restrict their existance to certain architectures
by providing an Architecture field with a list of architectures. Other
fields are propagated verbatim to britney, eg. Provides.

As for the second kind, you don't directly create faux packages, but
just append the packages which shouldn't be removed to the appropriate
file under the "noremove.d" directory. Those files just contain a list
of *binary* packages, one per line. A couple of important notes:

  - if the binary package is not in testing, do not add it: wait until
    it migrates.

  - if the binary package is not available on i386 (being arch:all would
    suffice), you have to qualify it with [arch], mentioning one arch in
    which it is available.

  - the names of the files under noremove.d must be valid as package
    names, e.g. not contain underscores.

The noremove.d/tasksel file is special: it gets generated automatically
by the fauxpkg.py script, by running the script with the "update-tasksel"
command. (Currently is it to be run by hand whenever the tasksel
maintainer notices our file is out of date.)

One further caveat: if one of these faux meta-packages becomes uninstallable,
they stop working (since the removal of their dependencies does not increase
the uninstallability count). The britney script has a mechanism in place to
warn in case they become uninstallable. When adding packages to the lists,
make sure to watch the next britney mail, in case you added conflicting
packages. (In that case, you want to add such packages in different files
under noremove.d.)

Please don't forget to commit your changes to files in this directory!
